ts. But we need to ensure we have exactly n distinct lines. Using vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1 gives n-1 lines. Then we need one more line (sunny) that covers the remaining points not covered by those vertical lines. Which points are not covered? The vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1 cover all points with x ≤ n-1. The only point not covered is (n,1). So we need a sunny line that passes through (n,1). But we also need to ensure that the line is distinct from the vertical lines (which are x=1,...,x=n-1). So any line through (n,1) that is not vertical (x=constant) is fine. But we also need to ensure that the line is not parallel to x-axis (horizontal) or slope -1. So we need a line through (n,1) with slope not 0, infinite, or -1. For example, line y = 2x - (2n-1) passes through (n,1) (since 1 = 2n - (2n-1) = 1). Its slope is 2, not 0, infinite, -1. So it's sunny. So k=1 works.\n\nNow k=2: exactly two sunny lines, n-2 non-sunny lines. The solution claims impossible. Let's examine. We need to see if there is a configuration with exactly two sunny lines. The solution's argument: Column x=n-2 has 3 points: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). Since a sunny line can cover at most one point per column (they claim), we need a non-sunny line to cover those three points. The only non-sunny lines that can cover multiple points in same column are vertical lines (x=constant). Horizontal lines cover points across rows, but they would be across columns. Slope -1 lines also cross columns. But the claim \"sunny lines cover at most one point per column\" is plausible: a line not vertical cannot have two points with same x-coordinate (since that would be vertical). So any non-vertical line (including sunny lines) can have at most one point per column. So to cover three points in column x=n-2, we need a vertical line x=n-2. So that vertical line is non-sunny. So we have one non-sunny line used.\n\nNow we have n-2 remaining lines (including the two sunny lines and n-4 other non-sunny lines). The solution then says analyzing columns x=n-1 and x=n shows that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). This forces a third sunny line, contradicting k=2.\n\nWe need to examine that argument. Let's see: After using vertical line x=n-2, we still need to cover points in columns x=n-1 and x=n. Let's list points:\n\n- Column x=n-1: points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) because a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ n+1-(n-1) = 2. So points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2).\n\n- Column x=n: point (n,1) only.\n\nNow we have n-2 remaining lines to cover these points. We have exactly two sunny lines allowed. The rest are non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/diag). We already used vertical line x=n-2. We can also use vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-3 (n-3 lines) as non-sunny lines. That would be n-3 vertical lines plus the vertical line x=n-2 = total n-2 vertical lines. That would be n-2 lines, covering all points except those in columns n-1 and n. But we have only n-2 lines left, but we need to cover points in columns n-1 and n. Actually we have n total lines: we used vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-3 (that's n-3 lines). Then we have vertical line x=n-2 (1 line). That's n-2 lines used. We have 2 lines left (since total n lines). Those two lines must be the sunny lines (since we need exactly k=2 sunny lines). So we have exactly two sunny lines left to cover points in columns n-1 and n.\n\nNow we need to see if we can cover points (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1) with two sunny lines. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, we need to assign points to lines such that each line covers at most one point in each column. So we have three points across two columns: column n-1 has two points, column n has one point. With two lines, we could assign one line to cover (n-1,1) and (n,1) maybe? But a line cannot have two points with different x coordinates? Actually a line can have points with different x coordinates; that's fine. But the line must pass through both points. So we need a line that passes through (n-1,1) and (n,1). That's a horizontal line y=1. But horizontal lines are non-sunny (parallel to x-axis). So that line would be non-sunny, not allowed as a sunny line. So we cannot use a sunny line to cover both (n-1,1) and (n,1). So we need to cover (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) maybe with one line? That would be vertical line x=n-1, which is non-sunny. So we cannot use a sunny line to cover both points in column n-1. So we need to cover each point individually with separate lines, or combine points across columns but not in same column. Let's see possibilities:\n\nWe have two sunny lines. They can each cover at most one point per column. So we could assign one sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and maybe also (n,1) if possible, but that line would be horizontal (non-sunny). So not allowed. Could we assign one sunny line to cover (n-1,2) and (n,1)? That line would have slope (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/(1) = -1. That's slope -1, which is non-sunny (parallel to x+y=0). So not allowed. Could we assign one sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and (n-1,2)? That's vertical, non-sunny. So not allowed. So we cannot cover any pair of these three points with a sunny line. So each sunny line can cover at most one of these points. Since we have three points and only two sunny lines, we cannot cover all three points. So we need a third sunny line or a non-sunny line. But we have used all non-sunny lines? Actually we could use a non-sunny line for one of these points. But we have used n-2 non-sunny lines already (vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-2). That's n-2 lines. We have total n lines, so we have exactly 2 lines left. If we want to use a non-sunny line for one of these points, we would need to replace a sunny line with a non-sunny line, but then we would have only one sunny line, not two. But we need exactly k=2 sunny lines. So we cannot use a non-sunny line for any of these points. So indeed k=2 is impossible.\n\nThus the argument seems valid.\n\nNow k=3: The solution says construction exists using three sunny lines. We need to see if we can construct with exactly three sunny lines. Let's try to find a construction. For n lines covering all points, we can use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (that's n-3 lines). Then we need to cover columns n-2, n-1, n with three lines. We have three lines left, which we can make sunny. We need to cover points in column n-2: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). Column n-1: (n-1,1), (n-1,2). Column n: (n,1). So we have total 6 points left. We have three lines to cover them. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, we need to assign points to lines such that each line covers at most one point per column. So we need to partition the 6 points into 3 lines, each line covering at most one point per column. That is possible: we can assign each line to cover two points, one from column n-2 and one from column n-1, and maybe also column n? Actually each line can have at most one point per column, but can have points from different columns. So we can try to assign:\n\n- Line A: (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) maybe? That line would have slope (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1/1 = 1. That's slope 1, which is sunny (since not 0, infinite, -1). So line A is sunny.\n\n- Line B: (n-2,2) and (n-1,1). Slope (1-2)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -1/1 = -1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. So we need to avoid slope -1. Let's try different pairing.\n\nWe need to find three sunny lines covering all six points. Let's try to use lines with slopes not 0, infinite, -1. We can also use lines that pass through (n,1). Let's try to assign:\n\n- Line 1: (n-2,1) and (n,1). That's horizontal line y=1, slope 0, non-sunny. Not allowed.\n\n- Line 1: (n-2,1) and (n-1,1). That's horizontal, not allowed.\n\n- Line 1: (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) slope 1, sunny.\n\n- Line 2: (n-2,2) and (n,1). Slope (1-2)/(n-(n-2)) = -1/2 = -0.5, which is not 0, infinite, -1. So sunny.\n\n- Line 3: (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). Slope (1-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -2/1 = -2, which is not -1, so sunny.\n\nNow we have covered all points: (n-2,1) used in line1, (n-2,2) used in line2, (n-2,3) used in line3, (n-1,2) used in line1, (n-1,1) used in line3, (n,1) used in line2. So three sunny lines cover all points. So k=3 works.\n\nThus answer {0,1,3} seems correct.\n\nNow we need to check if any other k values possible? The solution claims k≥4 impossible because sunny lines cover at most one point per column. Let's examine: If we have k≥4 sunny lines, we have at most n-k non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. The vertical lines can cover many points in a column. Horizontal lines can cover many points in a row. Slope -1 lines can cover many points along a diagonal. But the constraint is that we have exactly n lines total. The points are arranged in a triangular shape. The maximum number of points per column is decreasing: column 1 has n points, column 2 has n-1 points, ..., column n has 1 point. So the column with the most points is column 1 (n points). If we have many sunny lines, they can each cover at most one point per column, so they can cover at most k points in column 1. The remaining points in column 1 must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines that can cover points in column 1 are vertical line x=1 (covers all n points), horizontal lines y=some constant (cover at most one point per column?), slope -1 lines also cover at most one point per column? Actually slope -1 lines are of form x+y=c. For column x=1, points (1,b) with b from 1 to n. The line x+y=c passes through (1,b) if b = c-1. So each slope -1 line can cover at most one point in column 1. So only vertical line x=1 can cover multiple points in column 1. So we need at least one vertical line x=1 to cover the rest of column 1 points not covered by sunny lines. So we need at least one vertical line. Similarly, for column 2, we need vertical line x=2 or other lines. But we can use vertical lines for many columns. The number of vertical lines needed is at most n-k (since we have n-k non-sunny lines). So we need to see if we can cover all points with n lines where k≥4. The solution claims impossible because sunny lines cover at most one point per column, and we have only n-k non-sunny lines to cover the rest. But we need to check if there is any scenario where k≥4 works. Let's try to see if we can have k=4 and n lines covering all points. For n≥3, we have n lines total. If k=4, then n-4 non-sunny lines. For n=3, k=4 is impossible because k cannot exceed n. So k≥4 only possible for n≥4. Let's test n=4, k=4. Then we have 4 sunny lines, 0 non-sunny lines. So all lines must be sunny. But each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. There are columns 1 to 4. Column 1 has points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). That's 4 points. With 4 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point in column 1, so we could cover all 4 points in column 1 with the 4 sunny lines, each covering a distinct point. However, we also need to cover points in other columns. Let's see: column 2 has points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). That's 3 points. We have only 4 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column, so we can cover at most 4 points in column 2, which is enough. But we need to assign each line to cover points across columns. However, each line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover multiple points across different columns. So we need to assign points to lines such that each line covers at most one point per column, and each point is covered by at least one line. With 4 lines, we need to cover total number of points = n(n+1)/2 = 4*5/2 = 10 points. Each line can cover at most one point per column, and there are 4 columns, so each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So total capacity of 4 lines is at most 4*4 = 16 points, which is enough. But we need to ensure distinct lines. Could we find 4 sunny lines that cover all 10 points? Let's try to construct. For n=4, points are:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2)\nColumn 4: (4,1)\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines. Let's try to assign each line to cover a set of points, one per column. For each line, we need to pick a point in each column (maybe not all columns). Since we have 4 lines, we can assign each point in column 1 to a distinct line. Then we need to assign points in column 2 to lines, possibly reusing lines that already have a point in column 1. But each line can have at most one point per column, but can have points in multiple columns. So we can assign each line to cover one point in column 1 and one point in column 2, etc. The question is whether we can assign all points without conflict. This is like a bipartite matching problem: we have lines as \"bins\" that can hold at most one point per column. Since we have 4 lines, we can assign up to 4 points per column. Column 1 has 4 points, okay. Column 2 has 3 points, okay. Column 3 has 2 points, okay. Column 4 has 1 point, okay. So capacity wise it's fine. But we need to ensure that each line's points are collinear (i.e., lie on a single line). That's the main difficulty: we need to find 4 lines such that each line passes through a set of points, one per column, and each point is covered. This is a combinatorial geometry problem. Is it possible? Let's try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points. For each line, we need to pick a set of points that are collinear. The points are integer lattice points. We need to find lines that are not vertical/horizontal/diag slope -1. Let's try to see if we can find 4 lines that cover all points. For n=4, maybe it's possible. Let's try to find a construction.\n\nWe can try to use lines of slope 1 (45-degree line) or other slopes. Let's list all points:\n\n(1,1)\n(1,2)\n(1,3)\n(1,4)\n(2,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,3)\n(3,1)\n(3,2)\n(4,1)\n\nWe need 4 lines. Let's try to see if we can partition these points into 4 collinear sets, each set containing at most one point per column.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of slope 1 passing through points (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) but (3,3) is not in the set because (3,3) has a+b=6 >5, so not present. So line y=x passes through (1,1),(2,2). That's two points. That's okay. That's a sunny line (slope 1). It covers column 1 point (1,1) and column 2 point (2,2). Good.\n\nAnother line: slope 2 maybe passes through (1,2) and (2,4) but (2,4) not present (2+4=6 >5). So not.\n\nLine slope 0.5 passes through (2,1) and (4,2) but (4,2) not present (4+2=6 >5). So not.\n\nLine slope -2 passes through (1,3) and (2,1) (since slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2). So line through (1,3) and (2,1) is y = -2x + 5? Let's compute: For x=1, y=3 => 3 = -2*1 + b => b = 5. So line y = -2x + 5. Check (3,? ) => y = -2*3 +5 = -1, not in set. So line covers (1,3) and (2,1). That's column 1 point (1,3) and column 2 point (2,1). That's okay. That's a sunny line (slope -2). Good.\n\nNow we have covered points (1,1),(2,2) and (1,3),(2,1). Still need to cover (1,2),(1,4),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 6 points left. We have 2 lines left (since total 4 lines). Each line can cover at most one point per column. So each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So total capacity left is 8 points, enough. But we need to find two lines covering these 6 points.\n\nLet's try line through (1,2) and (3,? ) maybe (3,? ) such that slope is something not 0, infinite, -1. Let's try line through (1,2) and (3,2) => slope (2-2)/(3-1)=0 => horizontal, not allowed. So not.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (3,1) => slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed (sunny). So line L3: passes through (1,2) and (3,1). Check if it also passes through (4,? ) maybe (4,0.5) not integer. So L3 covers (1,2) and (3,1). That's column 1 point (1,2) and column 3 point (3,1). Good.\n\nNow remaining points: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). That's 4 points left, and we have one line left. Can we find a line that passes through all four? Probably not. But we can try to see if we can cover them with one line. Let's see if they are collinear: (1,4) and (2,3) slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1 => slope -1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. So we cannot have a sunny line covering both (1,4) and (2,3). Could we have a line covering (1,4) and (3,2)? Slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1 => slope -1, not allowed. So not.\n\nCould we have a line covering (1,4) and (4,1)? Slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So any line connecting (1,4) to any other point in column >1 seems to have slope -1? Let's check (2,3) to (4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, again. (3,2) to (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1/1 = -1. So all points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1) lie on the line x+y=5, which is slope -1, non-sunny. So they cannot be covered by a sunny line. So we need a non-sunny line to cover them. But we have used all non-sunny lines (vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-3? Actually we used vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-3? Let's see: For n=4, we used vertical lines x=1? Actually we used vertical lines? In our construction we used only sunny lines so far: L1 (y=x) slope 1, L2 (y=-2x+5) slope -2, L3 (through (1,2) and (3,1)) slope -0.5. That's 3 sunny lines. We have one line left, which we need to be sunny (since k=4). But we need to cover points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1) which are all on a non-sunny line (slope -1). So we cannot cover them with a sunny line. So k=4 fails for n=4.\n\nThus k≥4 seems impossible for n=4. For larger n, similar reasoning may hold: the points on the line x+y=n+1 (the \"hypotenuse\" of the triangle) are all on a line of slope -1, which is non-sunny. So to cover those points, we need either a non-sunny line (the line itself) or multiple sunny lines each covering at most one point from that line. Since there are n points on that line (points (i, n+1-i) for i=1..n). If we have k sunny lines, they can cover at most k points from that line (one per line). The remaining n-k points must be covered by non-sunny lines. But we have only n-k non-sunny lines total (since total lines = n). So we could use each non-sunny line to cover one point from that line. However, non-sunny lines can be vertical/horizontal/diag slope -1. The line x+y=n+1 is itself a non-sunny line (slope -1). So we could use that line as one non-sunny line to cover all n points on that line. That would be a single non-sunny line covering all points on the hypotenuse. So we could have k up to n-1? Wait, we need to cover all points, not just the hypotenuse. Let's think more systematically.\n\nThe set of points includes the hypotenuse points (i, n+1-i) for i=1..n. That's n points. If we use the line x+y=n+1 (slope -1) as a non-sunny line, it covers all those n points. Then we need to cover the rest of the points (the interior of the triangle). The interior points are those with a+b ≤ n (strictly less). Those are points not on the hypotenuse. For each column i, there are points (i,b) for b=1..n+1-i. The hypotenuse point is (i, n+1-i). So interior points in column i are (i,b) for b=1..n-i. So column i has n-i interior points. So total interior points = sum_{i=1}^{n} (n-i) = sum_{j=0}^{n-1} j = n(n-1)/2. So total points = n + n(n-1)/2 = n(n+1)/2, as before.\n\nNow if we use the line x+y=n+1 as a non-sunny line, we have used 1 non-sunny line. We have n-1 lines left, of which k must be sunny. So we have n-1-k non-sunny lines left. We need to cover interior points with these lines. The interior points are arranged in a triangular shape of size n-1 (i.e., points (a,b) with a,b≥1 and a+b ≤ n). So it's the same problem but with n replaced by n-1. So we need to cover interior points with n-1 lines, exactly k sunny lines (since we used one non-sunny line). So the problem reduces to the same problem for n-1 with k sunny lines. So we have a recurrence: existence for (n,k) implies existence for (n-1,k) (if we use the hypotenuse line). Conversely, if we have a solution for (n-1,k), we can extend to (n,k) by adding the hypotenuse line as a non-sunny line. So the set of possible k for n is the same as for n-1 (since we can 